{"parentUuid":"59aa0075-61c1-4176-93de-27fe16600a30","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a48375ff23bdc6dbe","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZQqHPFjzubuKJidWaCH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ThEjFxYtWeVQYMR3ScVvM8","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework; ls docs data; git log --oneline | head -3"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1231,"cache_read_input_tokens":50042,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1231,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01ThEjFxYtWeVQYMR3ScVvM8":{"command":"cd /home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework; ls docs data; git log --oneline | head -3"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfZQqGvDtZfzDA1BmJSsr","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"5d26c49c-8a42-4578-a423-387343f445d5","timestamp":"2026-09-30T09:46:59.489Z","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1","slug":"warm-pondering-kernighan"}
{"parentUuid":"59aa0075-61c1-4176-93de-27fe16600a30","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"dd57ce8b-c203-427b-8b55-fa55e6dd1984","agentId":"a48375ff23bdc6dbe","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014WfvxCMofS1HS8oyPD8STm","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t===== K-ARCH-2 skills.json profiles/targets, configurations minimal-client and online-3d-server, reference-games\n2\tF: Scale-down is proven only as closure, not minimality. Membership is profile 'all' or match, and most skills are tagged all, so minimal-client (no physics UI/card game) has 74 skills vs 98 for standard-3d-client, including anti-cheat-integrity, cinematics-sequencer, vfx-particles, media-playback, animation-graphs, ai-behavior-perception, reconstruction-upscaling and reference-games. online-3d-server includes cloth-deformables, motion-synthesis, vegetation-foliage, atmosphere-weather, water-ocean, localization-i18n.\n3\tP: Give configurations explicit feature roots and add a reachability check (every member reachable from roots or universals); retag anti-cheat to online add-ons, cinematics/vfx/media/ai/animation-graphs to opt-in profiles, drop server target from cosmetic skills unless a headless capability exists; make reference-games a non-build kind.\n4\tV: The counts (74 vs 98 members, cosmetic skills tagged 'all' or server) are accurate, but docs/06 section C item 4 records that membership means 'may be built, not must be built' and that the reference game proves use. The finding contradicts that reasoned scope decision without new evidence.\n5\t===== K-ARCH-4 PHY.ARCH.validation, ANM.ARCH.validation, AUD.ARCH.validation, GAM.AI.validation, RES.MGMT.validation, UI.FW.validation vs QA.SIM.stability-suite, QA.SIM.animation, QA.SIM.audio\n6\tF: Oracle suites are owned twice: physics/animation/audio 'oracle suite' capabilities belong to the implementing leads while QA.SIM.* own the same metrics and simulation-validation is the declared oracle_author of C-PHYS/C-ANIM/C-AUDIO. GAM.AI.validation and RES.MGMT.validation put suites with implementers while C-NAV/C-RES name another oracle author. Unlike RND.MAT.validation they lack the 'hooks only' qualifier, contradicting the implementer-vs-oracle-author independence rule.\n7\tP: Reword all *.validation capabilities as hooks/reference scenes/threshold proposals with suite authorship by the oracle_author; add QA.SIM.navigation and QA.SIM.streaming (or reassign C-NAV/C-RES oracle authors); add a check that owner-equals-implementer oracle capabilities carry the hooks-only marker.\n8\tV: Confirmed: PHY/ANM/AUD.ARCH.validation are 'oracle suite' capabilities owned by the implementing leads, while C-PHYS/C-ANIM/C-AUDIO name simulation-validation as oracle_author and QA.SIM.* own the same metrics. GAM.AI.validation and RES.MGMT.validation lack the 'hooks only' qualifier that RND.MAT.validation and RND.RHI.conformance carry, and C-NAV/C-RES name other oracle authors. check.py only tests contract-level independence, so the rewording is a sound fix.\n9\t===== K-ARCH-5 robustness-fuzzing, functional-automation-soak, simulation-validation, render-validation (oracle_author of 111 code contracts)\n10\tF: Four process skills author every conformance suite: functional-automation-soak 35 contracts, robustness-fuzzing 26, simulation-validation 26, render-validation 24. robustness-fuzzing (5 capabilities) is oracle author for C-MATH, C-SYNC, C-TASK, C-MEM, C-ECS, C-CFG, C-CRASH, C-ML; functional-automation-soak for C-TEXT, C-LOC, C-BUILD, C-VCS, C-EDCMD. This breaks the distinct-expertise rule and serializes milestone freezes behind four agents.\n11\tP: Add experts foundation-conformance (memory model, math reference, containers, allocator, config, ECS model tests) and ui-text-conformance (shaping, CLDR, layout, focus) under test-architect and re-point the affected contracts; keep robustness-fuzzing for parsers and fault campaigns.\n12\tV: Oracle-author counts confirmed (35/26/26/24 = 111), including robustness-fuzzing for C-MATH/C-SYNC/C-MEM/C-ECS and functional-automation-soak for C-TEXT/C-LOC/C-BUILD/C-VCS. This is expertise concentration on process skills across unrelated literatures. The staffing-serialization claim is weaker, but the split proposal is sound.\n13\t===== K-ARCH-6 ARCH.ORG.ownership-ledger, ARCH.ORG.agent-continuity, ARCH.ORG.write-sets, ARCH.ORG.provenance, BLD.CI.sealed-suites, ARCH.GOV.fitness, program-orchestration, C-ORCH\n14\tF: The coordination machinery is software (lease/lock server with fencing tokens, per-path access-class enforcement incl. sealed:oracle, work queue, provenance store, fitness-function runner) but is owned by process skills, which docs/00 says cannot own code; no tool or runtime skill builds, tests or operates it, and it is labelled emerging.\n15\tP: Add a tool-kind expert orchestration-infrastructure owning ledger/lease service, ACL enforcement in VCS/CI, work queue, provenance store and fitness runner; split C-ORCH into a process protocol and a code contract with chaos-test oracle; include it in M0.\n16\tV: Partly wrong: BLD.CI.sealed-suites is owned by the tool skill ci-cd-automation, and C-ORCH is already frozen in M0. Coordination is a recorded emerging radar entry with a human-coordinator fallback (docs/09), so 'include in M0' and the wrong-owner framing do not hold.\n17\t===== K-ARCH-8 NET.PRED.lagcomp, PHY.ARCH.rewind, ANM.RT.pose-history, GAM.SYS.hit-detection, GAM.SYS.projectiles, anti-cheat-integrity, C-PREDICT\n18\tF: Lag compensation/server rewind is split across four skills (collider history in physics, pose history in animation, window and protocol in prediction-rollback, hurtbox resolution and rewind validation in gameplay toolkit) with no contract naming the composite 'hit volumes with poses as of tick T'; consistency, memory cost per tick and fairness policy are unowned.\n19\tP: Add NET.PRED.rewound-world-query and contract C-REWIND (L3) with collider-history and pose-history providers implemented by physics and animation-runtime, consumed by C-ABILITY and C-INTEGRITY; oracle author simulation-validation.\n20\tV: Collider history sits in C-PHYS and pose history in C-ANIM, and C-PREDICT says only 'rewind queries'. No composite contract exists, so the gap is real. However NET.PRED.lagcomp gives prediction-rollback a single owner for the subsystem, and it already reaches both providers through optional edges. That makes this a moderate gap and 'major' looks inflated.\n21\t===== K-ARCH-12 engine-architect, ARCH.ORG.escalation, C-A11Y\n22\tF: engine-architect has 29 direct children and is sole arbiter for intra-workstream, cross-workstream and party-lead disputes with no workstream coordinator; also C-A11Y is the only contract never frozen because check.py requires freezes only for code contracts.\n23\tP: Designate a workstream coordinator (non-party lead) as first-instance arbiter and reserve engine-architect for cross-workstream cases; freeze C-A11Y at M1 and extend the never-frozen check to named process contracts.\n24\tV: engine-architect has 29 direct children (the maximum) and is the sole arbiter. C-A11Y is the only contract never frozen; check.py skips layer-P contracts, and other P contracts such as C-ORCH are frozen. Minor and valid, though the proposed coordinator adds process weight.\n25\t===== K-COMPLETE-5 PLAT.LIVE.events, UI.LOC.cldr, PLAT.SVC.trusted-time\n26\tF: Time-gated events, daily/weekly resets and seasons have no owned calendar semantics (UTC vs local vs region time, DST, tzdata delivery on frozen platforms); timezone/calendar appears nowhere.\n27\tP: Extend PLAT.LIVE.events or add PLAT.LIVE.calendar owning time-zone/DST policy and tzdata delivery via remote config, with trusted-time as authority.\n28\tV: Timezone/DST is absent, but PLAT.LIVE.events already owns time-gated activation with trusted-time as authority, so calendar/tzdata policy is an implementation detail of an existing capability rather than a framework-level ownership gap.\n29\t===== K-FUTURE-2 data/radar.json; scripts/check.py radar rules; ARCH.GOV.radar\n30\tF: Radar evidence and triggers are time-relative prose ('preview', 'beta', 'no shipped titles') with no as-of date, cited sources or staleness check, so maturity labels cannot be shown honest over a multi-year program and cannot be caught when stale. Labels such as cooperative-vector preview and work-graph 'no shipped titles' will flip within months, and radar status gates profile retagging.\n31\tP: Add required fields reviewed (ISO date), evidence_refs (dated, typed T1-T6 sources) and per-class max_age_days (e.g. X/S 180, M 365); make check.py fail entries older than max age relative to the milestone date; express promotion triggers as testable predicates (shipped-title count, API status).\n32\tV: radar.json entries have only tech/class/evidence/revisit/fallback with no as-of date, sources or staleness rule, and check.py has no age check; time-relative labels that gate retagging are a real long-horizon defect and the proposal is sound.\n33\t===== K-FUTURE-3 PLAT.PAL.cloud-streaming; PLAT.PAL.cloud-hybrid; skills.json target/platform axes; C-PRESENT\n34\tF: Streaming-first delivery is modelled only for a build being streamed (session detection, touch overlay, UI scale). The engine-as-cloud-render-host case (headless GPU rendering, frame capture, low-latency hardware encode, video/input back-channel, multi-tenant GPU packing) has no owner or radar entry. headless-client is a non-rendering bot target, PLAT.SVC.capture is clip export, and encode appears only under hetero-offload (X).\n35\tP: Add capability PLAT.PAL.cloud-render-host (platform-architect, M; contributors frame-orchestration, gpu-platform-architect, network-transport, input-system), a radar entry (fallback: client build streamed by third-party host), a cloud-render-host target/platform value, and an encoder sink among C-PRESENT interposed presenters.\n36\tV: Only PLAT.PAL.cloud-streaming (streamed-build policy) and cloud-hybrid exist, with no cloud-render-host capability, radar entry or target, and encode appears only as RND.MEDIA.encode and hetero offload; a real omission not covered by the backend-services non-goal.\n37\t===== K-FUTURE-7 non-goal GLES/WebGL backends; indie-2d configurations on web; RND.RHI.webgpu\n38\tF: Smallest 2D/card games claim web yet the web target requires WebGPU and the WebGL non-goal has no fallback; a sprite renderer needs none of WebGPU. The revisit trigger (>10% of a shipping audience lacking WebGPU) can only fire after shipping with no fallback; older WebViews and embedded browsers lag for years.\n39\tP: Keep WebGL out for std3d but allow a reduced-tier registered backend slot (or Canvas2D/WebGL2 path in render-2d-vector) for minimal/min2d web builds; or convert the trigger to a pre-ship gate using PLAT.WEB.audience-share at the web-release milestone.\n40\tV: The WebGL non-goal and its measured-share revisit trigger (PLAT.WEB.audience-share) are a recorded, reasoned scope decision in docs/00 and radar, and the finding adds no new evidence beyond the general WebGPU rollout tail.\n41\t===== K-GAMEPLAY-6 facial-animation (profiles lite3d/std3d); ANM.FACE.lipsync; ANM.RT.2d; narrative-dialogue; minimal and min2d configurations\n42\tF: FACE.lipsync (per-locale) lives only in facial-animation, tagged lite3d/std3d. The minimal profile (narrative-centric) and min2d carry VO, C-DIALOGUE and per-locale VO packs but have no audio-driven mouth/expression driving for 2D portraits and sprites, and no portrait/expression-layer staging or text reveal synced to VO. Visual novels and 2D RPGs would rebuild this per game, and per-locale retiming is unsolved for them.\n43\tP: Add ANM.FACE.lipsync-2d (owner facial-animation with profile addition for min2d and minimal, or owner animation-runtime; contributors audio-content-runtime, narrative-dialogue) and GAM.NARR.staging-2d (portrait/expression layers, text reveal timing). Widen or split the facial-animation profiles.\n44\tV: The premise that the minimal profile is narrative-centric and carries VN needs is not evidenced in the data or docs, and portrait staging and text reveal are game-level content (docs/06 C.2 scopes game-specific systems out), so major severity is inflated.\n45\t===== K-LEGACY-2 legacy-patterns.json (new pattern), RND.GEO.precomputed-visibility, RND.GI.baked, RND.GI.lightmap-uv, RND.GI.bake-pipeline\n46\tF: No catalogue entry covers Quake-lineage static-world assumptions. Baked PVS/portal visibility and lightmap-only lighting are plain E capabilities with no tier restriction or stance. They conflict with destruction, time of day, runtime PCG and UGC worlds.\n47\tP: Add pattern L75 'Baked static visibility/lighting as the world model'. Stance: dynamic GI and GPU/HZB occlusion on capable tiers, baked as a per-tier fallback with dynamic-object and invalidation rules (RND.ARCH.invalidation). Owner render-architect. stance_capabilities RND.GI.baked and RND.GEO.precomputed-visibility. Reword both capabilities as tier fallbacks.\n48\tV: Baked GI/PVS exist as E capabilities, but docs/06 B5 already records 'baked stays for low tiers', dynamic GI and GPU HZB occlusion capabilities already exist, and using the baked capabilities as stance for an anti-baked pattern is incoherent, so at most a wording nit, not major.\n49\t===== K-LEGACY-3 legacy-patterns.json (new patterns), CORE.JOBS.thread-model, GAM.SAVE.atomic, CORE.FRAME.time\n50\tF: Catalogue gaps with only implicit stances. (a) Thread-per-subsystem or per-connection blocking threads: L03 covers only game/render. (b) Wall-clock or sleep-based gameplay timers (L57 covers only tests). (c) Compiled-in fixed capacity caps. (d) Synchronous save or autosave that hitches the frame (L64 covers only the schema).\n51\tP: Add entries with owners, stance_capabilities and contradiction_terms. 'Fixed per-subsystem or per-connection threads' -> pool plus pinned lanes (job-system-task-graph, CORE.JOBS.thread-model). 'Wall-clock gameplay timers' -> game-time timers on time domains (CORE.FRAME.time). 'Compiled-in capacity caps' -> budget-declared, config-sized pools (CORE.MEM.budget-enforcement). 'Synchronous save/autosave' -> async atomic snapshot save (GAM.SAVE.atomic).\n52\tV: Four unrelated gaps are bundled and each is partly covered (L03/CORE.JOBS.thread-model for threads, L65/L16/L66 for sleeps, blocking IO and budgeted allocation), and they are catalogue-completeness wishes rather than a major defect.\n53\t===== K-LEGACY-7 WLD.MODEL.document, WLD.MODEL.travel, C-PCG\n54\tF: 'World / level document format', 'Level transitions & seamless travel' and C-PCG 'per cell/chunk/level' keep the level as an authoritative unit, while WLD.MODEL.strategy, L18 and L39 stance the world as partitioned data. Level-as-file can re-enter as the default authoring and travel unit for 3D.\n55\tP: Rename to 'World document (unit or partitioned)' and 'World-unit transition & seamless travel'. Add 'level' to L39 contradiction_terms, with an exception for minimal 2D configurations (WLD.MODEL.unit-load).\n56\tV: L39, L18, L07 and WLD.MODEL.strategy/file-per-object already stance partitioned worlds, the renames are cosmetic, and adding the bare term 'level' to contradiction_terms would false-positive on many unrelated capability and skill texts, so the proposal is unsound.\n57\t===== K-LEGACY-8 CORE.ECS.singletons, C-ECS, L32\n58\tF: 'Shared / singleton components & resources' mirrors the hidden-global pattern. Nothing states that singletons are world-scoped, and CORE.ECS.singletons is not an L32 stance capability.\n59\tP: Reword to 'World-scoped shared components/resources (no process-global singletons)'. Add the capability to L32 stance_capabilities.\n60\tV: CORE.OBJ.world-instances already states world-scoped state reachable only through a world handle with no current-world global and L32 stances it, so ECS singletons are already covered and the finding is a naming preference.\n61\t===== K-NET-1 milestones M2/M4; C-REP; C-PREDICT; C-HOSTAUTH; C-NETSESSION\n62\tF: C-REP, C-PREDICT, C-NETSESSION and C-HOSTAUTH are frozen at M2, but no configuration claimed by M2 contains replication; the first configurations with replication, lag compensation, server-authoritative reconciliation, listen and dedicated hosting are claimed at M4. The contracts are frozen before their real consumers exist. check.py only proves that an owner and some consumer skill exist, not that a claimed configuration exercises the contract.\n63\tP: Freeze C-REP at M4. Split the C-PREDICT freeze: rollback and lockstep at M2, server-authoritative reconciliation and lag compensation at M4. Or add a server-authoritative configuration to M2. Add a check that a contract is frozen only at a milestone claiming a configuration containing its owner and a consumer.\n64\tV: Only C-REP lacks an M2 configuration (replication is profile 'online' only); C-PREDICT, C-NETSESSION and C-HOSTAUTH are exercised at M2 by fighting-2d-rollback-client (online-rollback contains prediction-rollback, net-session and transport), the ladder intentionally drafts at M1 and freezes at M2, check.py already enforces owner/consumer skills before freeze, and the proposed C-PREDICT split contradicts the 'frozen exactly once' rule.\n65\t===== K-NET-6 configurations; dedicated-server profiles; NET.SESS.host-mode; NET.PRED.spectator; QA.FUNC.network\n66\tF: No configuration has a server or headless target for lockstep or rollback; dedicated-server, replication and server-scaleout carry only the 'online' profile. rts-*, fighting-2d-rollback-* and lockstep tools are client/tools only. Relay-hosted or referee hosting, server-side desync arbitration, server-run input spectators, replay verification and a headless bot for the M2 rollback and M4 2-hour lockstep soak are not proven closed; online-3d-bot-client exists only for std3d.\n67\tP: Add rts-2d-lockstep-server (min2d, massim, online-lockstep, server) and fighting-2d-rollback-headless-client (min2d, online-rollback, headless-client). Give dedicated-server the online-lockstep and online-rollback profiles or a slim relay/referee variant. Claim them at M2/M4.\n68\tV: The configuration facts are true, but docs/00 records a reasoned decision that input-only netcode families are separate add-ons not linked to state replication/servers, relay operation is external:backend, and lockstep/rollback soaks can run under QA.SIM.netsim, so adding server/headless configurations and dedicated-server profiles is a scope expansion without new evidence.\n69\t===== K-NET-7 NET.PRED.lagcomp; NET.REP.interpolation; C-INPUT; C-PREDICT; C-REP; PHY.ARCH.rewind; ANM.RT.pose-history\n70\tF: Lag compensation needs the client view time (interpolation tick pair and delay) at fire time. Replication produces it, C-INPUT frames must carry it, prediction-rollback consumes it, C-PHYS collider history and C-ANIM pose history are queried with it. No contract carries it: C-INPUT is only tick-stamped command frames, C-PREDICT no longer requires C-REP, and the composed rewound query is split across three contracts with no owner. No rewind-window/memory budget line exists.\n71\tP: Add an extensible view-stamp field to C-INPUT command frames. C-REP exposes client view time. Add a C-PREDICT rewound-query facade composing C-PHYS and C-ANIM histories. Add a rewind window and memory line to C-BUDGET per configuration.\n72\tV: Overstated: C-PREDICT already lists rewind queries and consumes C-PHYS?/C-ANIM?, NET.PRED.lagcomp is owned by prediction-rollback (so the composed query has an owner), and C-PREDICT no longer requires C-REP by a recorded decision; only a view-stamp field detail and a rewind-memory budget line are absent, which is minor.\n73\t===== K-NET-11 ANM.RT.pose-history; NET.PRED.lagcomp; indie-2d-online-moddable-server\n74\tF: ANM.RT.pose-history is restricted to lite3d/std3d, but min2d online configurations contain prediction-rollback with lag compensation; animated 2D hitboxes (skeletal 2D, frame-data sprites) have no history owner.\n75\tP: Add min2d to the capability profiles or add a 2D hitbox-history obligation in physics-2d via PHY.ARCH.rewind.\n76\tV: ANM.RT.pose-history is indeed restricted to lite3d/std3d, but PHY.ARCH.rewind already covers collider history, GAM.SYS.hit-detection owns frame-data hitboxes with a prediction-rollback contributor, and frame-data hitboxes are state-derived, so this niche 2D server-rewind case is not a material defect.\n77\t===== K-PERF-3 PRF.METH.asymptotics, PRF.BENCH.scale-content, milestones M1/M5/M6, C-FRAME, C-FLOW, C-SPATIAL, C-VIEW, C-SIGNIF\n78\tF: Structural contracts that fix world-scale asymptotics are frozen at M1 on 2D indie games, but asymptotic declarations and stress worlds are first gated at M5/M6. Their validators are circular: M5 gates scale-content validated by asymptotics, and M6 gates asymptotics validated by scale-content. A scaling flaw found at M5 forces a breaking change to frozen contracts consumed by most skills. No scale probe runs before freeze.\n79\tP: Add an M1 gate for PRF.METH.asymptotics (declaration) validated by PRF.BENCH.scale-content, plus an M1 scale-probe (1e6 entities, 1e4 cells, 64 cores, fitted slopes) as a precondition to freezing C-FRAME, C-FLOW, C-SPATIAL, C-VIEW and C-SIGNIF. Break the M5/M6 circularity by giving scale-content an independent oracle. Make 'asymptotic line declared' a freeze condition for every contract.\n80\tV: Confirmed: C-FRAME/C-FLOW/C-SPATIAL/C-VIEW are frozen at M1 (C-SIGNIF at M2), while M5 gates scale-content validated by asymptotics and M6 gates asymptotics validated by scale-content, so the validators are circular and no scale probe precedes freeze.\n81\t===== K-PERF-7 milestones M2/M3/M4/M5, PRF.METH.energy, PRF.NET.resim-cost, PRF.MEM.size, PRF.NET.latency, PRF.NET.server-density\n82\tF: Milestone exits and gates are inconsistent with claimed obligations. M4 exit says server density is within budget, but PRF.NET.server-density is gated only at M6. Rollback at M2 has no PRF.NET.resim-cost gate. lite-3d mobile (M3) and xr-standalone (M5) have no energy or sustained-thermal gate. PRF.MEM.size (WASM and download size) and PRF.NET.latency are never gated.\n83\tP: Add gates: M2 PRF.NET.resim-cost (validator QA.SIM.netsim), M2 PRF.MEM.size (PRF.BENCH.stats), M3 PRF.METH.energy (PRF.GPU.power), M4 PRF.NET.server-density and PRF.NET.latency (QA.FUNC.load), M5 PRF.METH.energy for xr-standalone. Keep M6 server-density for the AAA point.\n84\tV: Verified in milestones.json: M4 exit claims server density in budget but PRF.NET.server-density is gated only at M6, and PRF.NET.resim-cost, PRF.MEM.size, PRF.NET.latency and PRF.METH.energy are never gated; minor severity is right.\n85\t===== K-PLATFORM-6 PLAT.SVC.*, platform-services, QA.CERT.store-policy, platform_variants.pc, BLD.REL.packaging, ARCH.REQ.platform-matrix\n86\tF: The platform matrix promises 'OS x ISA x API x store', but platform_variants has no store axis. No capability owns PC storefront SDK adapters (Steamworks, Epic Online Services, GOG Galaxy, Microsoft Store/GDK-PC), per-store build variants and depots, or Steam Input and overlay rules. platform-services is defined as first-party only.\n87\tP: Add PLAT.SVC.pc-storefronts (E, platform-services, contributors platform-desktop and packaging-release-patching) and BLD.REL.store-variants. Add a store field to configurations or platform_variants.\n88\tV: ARCH.REQ.platform-matrix promises a store axis, but platform_variants has none and no capability covers PC storefront adapters or per-store build variants. platform-services is defined as first-party only, so the omission is real.\n89\t===== K-PLATFORM-8 UI.A11Y.screen-reader, UI.TXT.ime, ARCH.STRUCT.platform-backends, platform-desktop, platform-mobile, platform-web\n90\tF: The backend-slot registry names an IME/a11y bridge, but only console has a named owner. For desktop, mobile and web the OS accessibility bridges (UIA, NSAccessibility, AT-SPI, UIAccessibility/TalkBack, and the ARIA/DOM shadow tree canvas apps need) sit in accessibility and text-fonts with platform-architect as a contributor. Platform implementers are not on the rows.\n91\tP: Add PLAT.DESK/MOB/WEB.a11y-ime-bridge rows (owner platform skill, contributors accessibility and text-fonts), or state explicitly that accessibility owns all non-console OS bridges.\n92\tV: UI.A11Y.screen-reader and UI.TXT.ime are owned by the domain skills with platform-architect as contributor. That is the framework's standard domain-owns, platform-contributes pattern, so this is an ownership preference rather than a defect.\n93\t===== K-PLATFORM-9 platform-server-host, PLAT.SRV.pal, rhi-vulkan, render-validation\n94\tF: platform-server-host is defined as 'no display or GPU'. Headless GPU hosts (Linux GPU nodes without a display, running offscreen render, GPU bake or texture encode, GPU cook, path-traced reference validation and cloud rendering) have no platform description, and rhi-vulkan does not declare server-host.\n95\tP: Add PLAT.SRV.headless-gpu (offscreen/headless surfaces, container GPU passthrough, driver pinning) and let rhi-vulkan declare server-host behind a headless flag.\n96\tV: platform-server-host is defined as having no GPU and rhi-vulkan omits server-host. The GPU-server cases are speculative: tools configurations run on pc and a null/software GPU device exists for headless CI, so no closure failure is shown.\n97\t===== K-PLATFORM-10 ML.RT.npu, ML.RT.os-models, C-ML, PLAT.PAL.capability-tiers\n98\tF: ML.RT.npu (NPU backends via platform ML APIs) is marked X although platform NPU paths (Core ML/Neural Engine, Windows ML/DirectML on Copilot+ PCs, Android accelerator APIs) ship, while ML.RT.os-models is M. C-ML has no platform implementer slot, so per-OS accelerator backends have no platform owner.\n99\tP: Split into ML.RT.npu-backends (M, contributors platform-desktop and platform-mobile) and keep in-frame NPU scheduling as X. Add accelerator discovery to PLAT.PAL.capability-tiers.\n100\tV: ML.RT.npu is X by a recorded, reasoned radar entry with a revisit trigger, and check.py ties radar class to capability maturity. Splitting it and adding a platform slot re-argues that decision without new evidence.\n101\t===== K-PROD-1 ARCH.PROD (new ARCH.PROD.support-policy), BLD.CI.backports, XC.EXT.versioning, C-RELEASE, M7\n102\tF: No capability owns the engine support commitment: LTS duration, N-1 support, end-of-support dates, platform-SDK/store-mandate sunset and licensee migration deadlines. BLD.CI.backports owns mechanics, XC.EXT.versioning owns API deprecation and PLAT.PAL.os-support-policy owns OS versions, but nobody decides how long a stream is supported. The ladder ends at M7 'first LTS' with no post-1.0 lifecycle. Searching capabilities for sunset/EOL/support-window finds nothing engine-side.\n103\tP: Add ARCH.PROD.support-policy (engine-product-management, E; contributors build-release-architect, api-lifecycle-migration, security-engineering) covering stream matrix, support window, EOL notice and platform-SDK sunset. Consume it from C-PROD/C-RELEASE. Add an M7 exit item 'EOL rehearsal of one release stream' and state that post-M7 work is cadence-driven.\n104\tV: XC.EXT.lts ('LTS & support policy', owned by api-lifecycle-migration) and BLD.REL.end-of-service already exist and are M7 gates, so the support commitment is covered and the 'nothing found' claim is wrong.\n105\t===== K-PROD-3 ARCH.PROD.customer-corpus, ARCH.PROD.intake, QA.REF.dogfood, milestones M2-M7\n106\tF: Every customer of the engine is agent-authored: reference games are written by the same organization against the public API and dogfood is internal. There is no external design-partner or early-access milestone, and intake/field-feedback have no real inflow until after M7. The API and its only validating games share the same blind spots.\n107\tP: Add ARCH.PROD.design-partners (engine-product-management, E) covering recruitment per profile, agreements, feedback SLAs and support tiers. Add gates: M2 one external indie title on the public API, M4 one external multiplayer team, M7 at least N external titles on the release candidate. Register partner agreements as human gates and extend the external-reports untrusted input to partner projects.\n108\tV: The absence of an external design-partner program is real, but making external titles milestone exit gates (M2/M4/M7) makes agent-executed milestones depend on outside parties and is a business-development request beyond the engine-skill framework's autonomous mandate, so it is unsound as proposed and at most minor.\n109\t===== K-PROD-7 ED.UI.ux, ED.ARCH.discipline-workflows, XC.DX.creator-docs, ARCH.ORG.human-gates\n110\tF: Editor usability and creator documentation are validated only by agent-runnable oracles (iteration targets, doc snippet tests). Creator experience for artists, designers and audio staff cannot be judged by agents, and no milestone or human gate samples real creators, so 'content creators as users' is unverified.\n111\tP: Add a human creator-panel sampling gate per discipline at M2 (indie tools), M3 and M6 (team-large tools) with task-completion and time-to-first-asset thresholds. Register it in ARCH.ORG.human-gates, with ED.UI.ux as metric owner and developer-experience-docs as contributor.\n112\tV: ED.UI.ux and creator-docs are validated only by agent-runnable oracles with no human creator sampling gate at any milestone, a small but real gap correctly rated minor.\n113\t===== K-RENDER-6 RND.ARCH.multiview, RND.ARCH.editor-rendering, ui-architect, GAM.CAM.photo, QA.RENDER.final-frame\n114\tF: Runtime offscreen render products are not owned: isolated render scenes with their own lighting and budget for 3D UI (character creator, inventory previews, portraits, icons), in-world render-to-texture (mirrors, security cameras, minimap), and player capture (photo-mode tiled high-res capture, UI-less and HDR screenshot output, platform share-capture restrictions). multiview mentions 'captures' in passing, preview-world in C-EDVIEW is editor-only, and QA final-frame capture is a test tool.\n115\tP: Add RND.ARCH.offscreen-scenes and RND.ARCH.capture owned by render-architect, contributors ui-architect, gameplay-camera and platform-services, and add a C-CERT entry for capture restrictions.\n116\tV: Partly covered already: RND.ARCH.multiview names PiP and captures, RND.ARCH.portals exists, GAM.CAM.photo is owned, and PLAT.SVC.capture ('Media capture & share integration') covers platform share-capture, so the claim that offscreen and capture products are unowned is overstated.\n117\t===== K-SEC-4 milestones.json M0, M2, M4; XC.SEC.agent-redteam; XC.SEC.dev-trust; XC.SEC.server-validation; XC.SEC.info-hiding; XC.SEC.incident; NET.SRV.admin; NET.SRV.transactions; PLAT.COMM.receipts\n118\tF: 22 of 30 XC.SEC capabilities are never named in a mile../../data:\n119\tcapabilities.json\n120\tcontracts.json\n121\tcritics.json\n122\tcrosscutting.json\n123\tlegacy-patterns.json\n124\tmilestones.json\n125\torganizations.json\n126\tradar.json\n127\tseed-map.json\n128\tskills.json\n129\tuntrusted-inputs.json\n130\t\n131\t../../docs:\n132\t00-design-principles.md\n133\t01-capability-map.md\n134\t02-skill-dependency-graph.md\n135\t03-skill-hierarchy.md\n136\t04-cross-cutting.md\n137\t05-critic-framework.md\n138\t06-gap-analysis.md\n139\t07-research-plan.md\n140\t08-phase-2-plan.md\n141\t09-radar-legacy-milestones.md\n142\t"}]},"uuid":"84941128-4fc8-4e74-9c89-680a3d1cfdb3","timestamp":"2026-09-30T09:46:59.505Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"59aa0075-61c1-4176-93de-27fe16600a30","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1","slug":"warm-pondering-kernighan"}
