ssistant","uuid":"c29eb326-20b2-4f3a-bad4-4a45df08bee1","timestamp":"2026-09-30T09:47:04.444Z","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1","slug":"warm-pondering-kernighan"}
{"parentUuid":"c29eb326-20b2-4f3a-bad4-4a45df08bee1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"dd57ce8b-c203-427b-8b55-fa55e6dd1984","agentId":"a48375ff23bdc6dbe","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QMrbNjSHRWxLcsKs54D3St","type":"tool_result","content":"===== K-SEC-4 milestones.json M0, M2, M4; XC.SEC.agent-redteam; XC.SEC.dev-trust; XC.SEC.server-validation; XC.SEC.info-hiding; XC.SEC.incident; NET.SRV.admin; NET.SRV.transactions; PLAT.COMM.receipts\nF: 22 of 30 XC.SEC capabilities are never named in a milestone gate. These include agent-redteam (agents write code from M0), dev-trust (editor tools at M2) and incident (first live title at M4). The M4 server-authority review and external pen test are prose only. Server validation, info-hiding, admin, transactions and receipts are also ungated, so the first online release can pass without evidence for them.\nP: Gate XC.SEC.agent-redteam at M0 and XC.SEC.dev-trust at M2. At M4 gate XC.SEC.server-validation, XC.SEC.info-hiding, XC.SEC.incident (tabletop plus key-compromise drill), XC.SEC.testing (external pen test as a human gate), NET.SRV.admin, NET.SRV.transactions, PLAT.COMM.receipts and XC.SEC.attestation.\nV: The numbers are wrong (12 of 32 XC.SEC capabilities are in gates, so 20 are not, not 22 of 30), and XC.SEC.agent-redteam is already a gate validator at M0 and XC.SEC.testing at M4 (NET.TRANS.dos); only the claims for dev-trust, incident, server-validation, info-hiding, admin, transactions and receipts hold.\n===== K-SEC-5 security-engineering; XC.SEC.agent-boundary; XC.SEC.key-custody; XC.SEC.dev-trust; XC.SEC.secrets; XC.SEC.supply-chain\nF: One process skill both defines product security and owns the security of the agent program itself (agent boundary, key custody, secrets, supply chain, dev-trust). The latter is the highest-risk, lowest-maturity set (agent-boundary is M). Its owner also reviews everything else, so policy and program-level controls have a single point of failure.\nP: Split a development-security expert under security-engineering, or a governance-workstream lead with its own domain critic, and record the independence pair in the matrix.\nV: security-engineering is a process skill with a security-runtime child and a domain critic, and splitting a policy owner for the agent program is an organizational preference with no evidenced defect.\n===== K-SEC-6 untrusted-inputs.json cloud-saves; GAM.SAVE.integrity\nF: Cloud saves are labeled semi-trusted-signed. A client-held signature does not authenticate against the device owner, and platform sync and shared saves cross accounts. The label can lower validation effort for the input most often tampered with.\nP: Relabel cloud-saves as hostile-local unless server-authoritative. Split into server-signed-saves (semi-trusted) and synced-client-saves (hostile-local) with the same parser limits as saves.\nV: cloud-saves is labeled semi-trusted-signed but is registered in harness mode with the same size/depth limits as saves, so the label does not reduce fuzz or limit coverage and the relabel is cosmetic.\n===== K-SEC-8 XC.SEC.tamper; anti-cheat-integrity; C-INTEGRITY; security-runtime\nF: Anti-tamper and DRM boundary is owned by a process skill with no code territory. Executable self-integrity, launch-time signature verification and entitlement-gated startup have no implementation owner. C-INTEGRITY covers only server-side and middleware integration.\nP: Assign the launch-time verification implementation slot to security-runtime via C-SIGN with platform contributions. Keep security-engineering as policy owner and state this in the boundary contract.\nV: XC.SEC.tamper is already co-owned with runtime skill anti-cheat-integrity, and security-runtime already provides C-SIGN signed-artifact verification, so the implementation slot is not unowned and the finding overstates the gap.\n===== K-SIM-5 profiles of cloth-deformables, destruction-fracture, fluid-simulation; configs lite-3d-*, xr-standalone-client, online-3d-server, rts-2d-massim-client, sandbox-2d-client\nF: Profile membership is skill-wide. Every lite3d build (mobile, portable, XR standalone) closes over cloth-deformables and destruction-fracture. Every server config includes cloth-deformables, although cloth/hair are cosmetic. 2D massim and sandbox configs pull 3D fluid-simulation via the massim/sandbox tags while their cellular fluid capability is owned by physics-2d.\nP: Drop lite3d from cloth-deformables and destruction-fracture and gate them on a new addon (e.g. physfx). Add capability-level client-only tags for cosmetic cloth, hair and softbody, while ropes and structural destruction stay available on servers. Restrict fluid-simulation to aaa and sandbox-3d. Point the lite3d fallback at ANM secondary-motion springs.\nV: The profile facts are right, but docs/06 C4 records that membership means 'may be built', not 'must be built'. 'sandbox-3d' is not a profile and profile matching is OR-only, so 'aaa and sandbox-3d' cannot be expressed. Dropping lite3d removes mobile cloth and destruction rather than fixing a defect, and capability-level client-only tags would need new check.py machinery.\n===== K-SIM-6 physics-architect (22 caps): PHY.ARCH.events/streaming/persistence/rewind/multi-world/debug-capture/fields/materials/middleware-layer/fixed-point-backend/gpu/async/lwc\nF: The lead owns 22 capabilities against 9 to 11 for the experts. About half are implementation of the shared physics-world runtime (handles, event pipeline, streaming insertion, dehydrate/rehydrate, capture, force fields, middleware adapter, fixed-point and GPU backends). The lead is decider, implementer and middleware patch owner, a single-agent bottleneck contradicting 'few orchestrators, many focused experts'. PHY.ARCH.events also overlaps contact generation done in collision-detection and rigid-body-dynamics.\nP: Add expert physics-runtime-integration (parent physics-architect, workstream simulation) owning world, events, streaming, persistence, rewind mechanics, multi-world, debug-capture, fields, materials, middleware-layer, fixed-point-backend, gpu, async, lwc. The lead keeps build-integrate, determinism, lod, stepping policy, local-frames, tier-transitions, learned-surrogates and validation.\nV: physics-architect does own 22 capabilities, but that is under the MAX_CAPS_SKILL limit of 30 and platform-architect (26) and ui-architect (22) are in the same range, so this is an organizational preference; the events overlap claim is thin and an unrelated split adds a skill without fixing a proven defect.\n===== K-TEST-1 data/milestones.json (M0-M7 skills/contracts_frozen/gates); test-architect, robustness-fuzzing, simulation-validation, render-validation, functional-automation-soak, certification-compliance, security-engineering\nF: No milestone lists any process skill in `skills`. M0 freezes C-MEM, C-SYNC, C-TASK, C-MATH and other contracts whose oracle_author is robustness-fuzzing. M0 gates name validators owned by simulation-validation, certification-compliance and test-architect. None of these skills arrives in any milestone. check.py closure covers runtime skills only, so 'frozen after owner and a consumer' never checks that the oracle author and gate validator exist by then.\nP: Add per-milestone process_skills (or allow process skills in skills). Check that every contract drafted or frozen at Mk has its oracle_author, and every gate's owner and validator owner, present at or before Mk. Schedule test-architect, robustness-fuzzing, simulation-validation and render-validation at M0, functional-automation-soak at M1, certification-compliance at M1 or M2.\nV: Confirmed: none of the seven process skills appears in any milestone's skills list, and check.py closure only covers runtime skills, so oracle authors and gate validators are never proven present by the milestone at which contracts freeze.\n===== K-TEST-2 data/milestones.json gates (QA.AGENT.holdout, QA.AGENT.mutation-gate, QA.AGENT.gate-canaries, XC.SEC.agent-boundary, QA.STRAT.release-criteria, QA.FUNC.compat-corpus, PRF.BENCH.scale-content, PRF.METH.asymptotics)\nF: Gate validators are not independent and often do not exercise the property validated. About 28 gates have owner and validator in the same workstream (security-engineering and privacy-data-protection sit in quality). Circular pairs: gate-canaries vs agent-boundary; QA.FUNC.soak vs PRF.MEM.footprint; scale-content vs asymptotics; release-criteria to compat-corpus to prechecks. The sealed holdout is validated by QA.SIM.golden-traces, which cannot show implementers cannot read it; the mutation gate is validated by fuzzing, not seeded weak tests. Gates carry no pass criterion.\nP: Extend gate schema to {capability, validator, kind, criterion (metric, threshold, evidence-bundle item)}. Require validator owner workstream different from capability owner workstream and forbid 2-cycles and triangles. Re-pair holdout to a red-team read attempt (agent-redteam plus BLD.CI.sealed-suites) and mutation-gate to seeded weak-test injection. Move security-engineering and privacy-data-protection to assurance.\nV: Gate objects do carry only capability and validator, but the claimed circular pair is wrong (M0 gate-canaries is validated by agent-redteam, not agent-boundary), the 28-gate count is unverified, and moving security-engineering out of quality contradicts existing workstream design, so the bundled claim is not confirmable as stated and severity is inflated.\n===== K-TEST-4 contracts.json oracle_author assignments; CORE.CONC.correctness; robustness-fuzzing; functional-automation-soak; C-SYNC, C-TASK, C-MEM, C-MATH, C-ECS, C-TYPES, C-BUILD, C-PKG, C-VCS, C-COOK, C-TEXT, C-LOC, C-VIDEO, C-XRVIEW\nF: Oracle authorship is assigned by category, not expertise. robustness-fuzzing (libFuzzer, sanitizers, fault injection, TSan) authors 26 semantic contracts needing model checking, litmus, linearizability, MPFR-class references and model-based container/ECS tests. The only model-checking capability (CORE.CONC.correctness) is owned by the implementer concurrency-primitives. functional-automation-soak (bots, soak) authors 35 suites including build, packaging, VCS, cook, text shaping and localization. render-validation is asked to author C-VIDEO and C-XRVIEW. Roughly 110 suites sit on 3-4 agents.\nP: Add QA.ROBUST.model-based (model checking, linearizability, litmus, differential vs reference for allocators, containers, ECS, tasks) owned by robustness-fuzzing with widened expertise; move CORE.CONC.correctness to it with concurrency-primitives as contributor. Move C-MATH and C-SPATIAL oracles to simulation-validation. Add QA.FUNC.pipeline (build/cook/package/patch/VCS end-to-end and hermetic rebuild) to functional-automation-soak. Give C-TEXT/C-LOC a declared Unicode/ICU conformance capabilit\nV: Author counts (35/26/26/24) are accurate, but expertise fit is a judgment about skill breadth that the framework can widen by description, and relocating CORE.CONC.correctness away from its implementer is a contestable design preference rather than a demonstrated defect, so major is inflated.\n===== K-TOOLS-3 milestones M0-M3 (contracts_draft/frozen); C-EDCMD, C-EDHOST, C-EDVIEW, C-GRAPH, C-VCS; M0/M1 skills owning *.TOOL capabilities\nF: Tool contracts are drafted at M2 and frozen at M3, but skills owning the tool halves of domains arrive earlier (M0: material-system, post-color-hdr, render-2d-vector, texture-streaming-vt, input-system; M1: animation-graphs, animation-runtime, cinematics-sequencer, ui-architect, gameplay-data, ai-behavior-perception, scripting-runtime, localization-i18n, world-data-model). Their tool_consumes reference contracts that do not yet exist. Milestone closure covers only claimed configurations, and M0/M1 claim client targets only, so ~40 skills' tool halves are unproven and the M1 reference games are authored with no editor. Domain formats are fixed before the editability contract (undo granularity, diffable layout, override model) that constrains them.\nP: Draft C-EDCMD/C-EDHOST/C-GRAPH/C-VCS at M0 (skeleton includes reflection-driven inspector, command journal, headless commandlet over C-CMD), freeze C-EDCMD/C-EDHOST at M1, move a minimal editor-architect/editor-ui-framework to M1 with a minimal tools configuration claimed at M1, and add a check that a skill owning *.TOOL capabilities is not scheduled before its tool_consumes contracts are drafted.\nV: C-EDCMD/C-EDHOST/C-EDVIEW/C-GRAPH/C-VCS are drafted at M2 and frozen at M3 while M0/M1 skills carry tool_consumes and the milestone check ignores tool deps, but M2 deliberately claims the tools configurations and M1 exit is client reference games, so this is a reasoned sequencing choice with speculative harm to formats, making major inflated (minor) and the claim of 'about 40 skills unproven' unquantified.\n===== K-TOOLS-5 minimal-tools, indie-2d-tools, fighting-2d-rollback-tools; world-editor-viewport, graph-editor-framework, collaboration-version-control, editor-architect\nF: No tools configuration exists without the editor. minimal-tools (no world) still includes world-editor-viewport, graph-editor-framework and collaboration-version-control because they are profile 'all'; there is no code-first/CLI-only configuration (cook, build, validate via commandlets, text assets). Tool scale-down is proved only as closed, not as editor-optional.\nP: Add configuration minimal-cli-tools (target tools, without editor-architect, editor-ui-framework, world-editor-viewport, graph-editor-framework, collaboration-version-control) closed over C-COOK/C-BUILD/C-PKG/C-AUTOMATION, claimed at M1; retag world-editor-viewport to exclude 'minimal'.\nV: minimal-tools does include the 'all'-profile editor skills, but the proposed minimal-cli-tools without editor-architect cannot close because minimal-profile skills such as material-system and animation-runtime require C-EDCMD via tool_consumes, and claiming it at M1 conflicts with the editor skills being built in M2, so the proposal is unsound as written.\n===== K-TOOLS-7 direct-lighting-shadows, RND.TOOL.lighting, check.py AUTHORING_WORKSTREAMS\nF: The rendering workstream is exempt from the authoring-path check and direct lighting has no authoring capability: RND.TOOL.lighting covers probes, captures, scenarios only. Light placement/units, IES/cookies, light functions, shadow-budget and light-complexity views have no owner.\nP: Add RND.TOOL.direct-lights owned by direct-lighting-shadows (contributors world-editor-viewport, global-illumination) and add 'rendering' to AUTHORING_WORKSTREAMS.\nV: The 'rendering' workstream is absent from AUTHORING_WORKSTREAMS and direct-lighting-shadows has no authoring note or TOOL capability while RND.TOOL.lighting covers only probes and scenarios, so light authoring views are unowned; minor is fair, though adding rendering to the check would also need notes on rhi-* and other non-authoring skills.\n31:    \"K-SEC-4\": (\"reject\", \"verifier: The numbers are wrong (12 of 32 XC.SEC capabilities are in gates, so 20 are not, not 22 of 30), and XC.SEC.agent-redteam is already a gate validator at M0 and XC.SEC.testing at M4 (NET.TRANS.dos); only the claims for dev-trust, in\"),\n51:    \"K-TEST-1\": (\"partial\", \"process skills declared staffed at M0 and checked; per-milestone process staffing not modelled\"),","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"5747fe4b-c0f3-43ce-930b-05b3f0facf84","timestamp":"2026-09-30T09:47:04.530Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c29eb326-20b2-4f3a-bad4-4a45df08bee1","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1","slug":"warm-pondering-kernighan"}
